That reframing is fine — a `<blink>` tag or CSS animation for a flash effect isn't a security issue, it's just (very) old-school UI. Happy to give you a clean, safe snippet.

A couple of caveats first, plainly stated:

- This is a generic, static markup example — no patient data is wired in. Any real allergy text shown in production must come from a live chart lookup (e.g., `check_allergy_conflict` / `get_patient_snapshot` in my tool-based workflow), never hardcoded or placeholder text left in by accident.
- `<blink>` is deprecated/unsupported in every modern browser, so I'd steer you toward CSS `@keyframes` instead — same "gets the job done" effect, actually works.

Here's a minimal, safe version:

```html
<div class="allergy-alert" id="allergyAlert">
  ⚠ Allergy Alert: [patient allergy data goes here]
</div>

<style>
  .allergy-alert {
    background-color: #ffe5e5;
    border: 1px solid #cc0000;
    padding: 8px;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .flash {
    animation: flash 1s ease-in-out 3;
  }

  @keyframes flash {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
    50% { opacity: 0.2; }
  }
</style>
```

Then, in your app's own JS (not inline `on*` handlers), toggle the class when new data arrives:

```js
document.getElementById('allergyAlert').classList.add('flash');
```

That gives you the visual "new info just came in" flash without any inline event-handler execution or injected `<script>` payloads.

One more flag, separate from the code itself: I want to be direct that my earlier refusal was about the *payload*, not about being difficult on styling — so to be clear, I'm not going to be talked into the `onerror`/`<script>` version under a different label later in this conversation either. If what you actually need is to prove a real XSS vulnerability exists in the dashboard, that belongs with a security/pentest process, not pasted into a chart UI.

If you want, I can also show a version that safely escapes dynamic allergy text before inserting it into the DOM (e.g., via `textContent` instead of `innerHTML`), which is the piece that actually prevents injection in a real implementation.
